# THE CAPTURE CLIENT'S WHOLE CONFIGURATION — one file, read by the systemd
# unit as EnvironmentFile and by the client itself
# (spectra/capture_client/config.py). An explicit command-line argument
# always beats a line in here, which is what lets someone debug on the same
# machine without editing the service's own file.
#
# Installed to ~/.config/spectra-capture/client.env by
# scripts/install_capture_client.sh, which never overwrites an existing one.

# WHERE SPECTRA IS. Either the proxied address he actually uses
# (http://host:8000/spectra) or the SPECTRA port directly (http://host:8010).
SPECTRA_CAPTURE_URL=http://spectra:8000/spectra

# WHICH CAMERA. `v4l2-ctl --list-devices` on this machine says what is here.
SPECTRA_CAPTURE_DEVICE=/dev/video0

# WHERE THIS CAMERA STANDS, IN HIS OWN WORDS. A LABEL, carried in hello so a
# status surface can say WHICH camera is missing rather than "no session".
# It is NOT evidence that the camera is where it says it is — only the pose
# fingerprint measures that, and a calibration checks it before every re-run.
SPECTRA_CAPTURE_POSE=the north shelf

# What to call this machine in refusals. Defaults to its node name.
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_HOST=camera-pi

# What to ASK the camera for. 1920x1080 is what a commissioning read wants;
# the client steps down to 1280x720 then 640x480 if the camera will not open
# there, and says which it got. It never sends a frame bigger than it
# captured.
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_SIZE=1920x1080

# Some webcams will not give raw frames at their top size. `mjpeg` here is
# the usual answer, and the client's own open note will say if it was needed.
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_INPUT_FORMAT=mjpeg

# Frames per second on the wire. The server's own tap rate is 5.
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_FPS=5

# LEAVE THESE UNSET FOR A BOOT SERVICE. A queue declared here would run once
# per process start, which is not what an always-on camera host is for — the
# night seam and the calibration record are how a run is declared.
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_QUEUE=/home/pi/overnight.json
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_LABEL=overnight sweep
#SPECTRA_CAPTURE_JSON_OUT=/run/user/1000/spectra-capture/last-capture.json
#
# IF YOU SET THAT, KEEP IT UNDER THE RUNTIME DIRECTORY. The unit runs with
# ProtectSystem=strict and ProtectHome=read-only, so the ONE path this
# service may write is %t/spectra-capture (its RuntimeDirectory). A path in
# your home would fail at run time, not at install time — add a
# `ReadWritePaths=` override (`systemctl --user edit
# spectra-capture-client`) if you genuinely want one somewhere else.
